 Run the tests with `python3 -m unittest`.
+
+## Why slugs are stable
+
+A slug is part of a published URL, so it is generated once and then stored.
+`slugify` is deliberately lossy — accents are folded, punctuation collapses —
+which means two different titles can produce the same slug. That is what
+`unique_slug` is for: pass it the slugs already taken in the section and it
+returns the first free variant.
+
+Never re-run `slugify` over an article that is already live. Regenerating a
+slug changes the URL, and the CMS does not keep a redirect.
+
+## Editorial conventions
+
+- Titles are slugified from the printed headline, not from the SEO title.
+- Section prefixes (`/espana/`, `/mercados/`) are added by the CMS and are not
+  part of what this helper returns.
+- Slugs longer than 80 characters are truncated by the CMS at publish time, so
+  keep headlines short if the tail matters.
